  "worked_solution": "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}\u20612\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u20612\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}\u20612\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}\u20612\\theta })^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u20612\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}\u20612\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{cos}\u20612\\theta )+\\frac{{sin}^{2}\u20612\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{cos}\u20612\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{cos}\u20612\\theta )^{2}+{sin}^{2}\u20612\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{cos}\u20612\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{2+2\\operatorname{cos}\u20612\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{cos}\u20612\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\text{\u00a0}\\,\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$",
